schema: concept-lineage
object:
  title: Phishing (crypto)
  type: concept-lineage
  short_description: >-
    Impersonating a trusted service to steal login details or private
    keys/seed phrases — via fake emails, typosquatted websites, DMs offering
    "help," or malicious browser extensions.
  source_traditions:
    - general information-security phishing terminology, applied to crypto wallets/exchanges
  toolkit_usage: >-
    Used as the first scam pattern in the article's field guide; the
    "golden rule" defense given is that no legitimate service ever asks for a
    seed phrase or private key.
  adjacent_meanings:
    - typosquatting
  important_distinctions:
    - >-
      phishing-vs-rug-pull — phishing steals credentials/keys from a victim;
      a rug pull is a project's own team absconding with pooled funds
  risks_of_flattening: >-
    Treating all phishing as identical hides real variation in vector (email
    vs. malicious browser extension vs. DM) that requires different specific
    defenses.
  maturity: raw
  ai_assisted: true
  provenance:
    origin: src/content/docs/common-scams.md
    transformation: summarized
    authorship: ai-assisted
